// A family applied at a call's result converts with itself. A type that reads
// a call's value runs the call first and binds it, so `tm(extend(g))` is
// `c ← extend(g); tm(c)`; a dependent arrow instantiated at the call
// `extend(g)` binds it ahead of each code that reads the binder, so the type
// it yields is written the same way (#720). The shape is a category with
// families: operations over context extension, laws that apply one operation
// to another's result (Dybjer, _Internal Type Theory_, TYPES 1995, LNCS
// 1158). The same law stated as a record's field joins once the record's
// family field prints at its reads (#728).

/// A family applied to an operation's result, met at one argument: `q(g)` is
/// at `tm(extend(g))`, which is `use`'s second domain at `extend(g)` (#720).
@[ checks ]
def useAtExtension(
  con : Type,
  tm : +U (con -> -F Type),
  extend : (g : con) => con,
  q : (g : con) => tm(extend(g)),
  use : (g : con, t : tm(g)) => Unit,
  g : con
) -> -F Unit {
  become use(extend(g), q(g))
}

/// The same with the family applied to a variable, which needs no call bound
/// ahead of it.
@[ checks ]
def useAtVariable(
  con : Type,
  tm : +U (con -> -F Type),
  q : (g : con) => tm(g),
  use : (g : con, t : tm(g)) => Unit,
  g : con
) -> -F Unit {
  become use(g, q(g))
}
